"StartLine\":\"101\",\"toolAction\":\"\\\"Viewing file\\\"\",\"toolSummary\":\"\\\"View antigravity_handoff.py linting rules\\\"\"}}]}\n148: {\"step_index\":149,\"source\":\"MODEL\",\"type\":\"GENERIC\",\"status\":\"DONE\",\"created_at\":\"2026-09-11T18:34:22Z\",\"content\":\"Created At: 2026-09-11T20:34:22+02:00\\nCompleted At: 2026-09-11T20:34:22+02:00\\nFile Path: `file:///mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/tools/antigravity_handoff.py`\\nTotal Lines: 348\\nTotal Bytes: 15551\\nShowing lines 101 to 250\\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\\n101: \\n102: # ── admissibility ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\\n103: #\\n104: # An answer is compiled as the *returned file*, helper lemmas included — pasting\\n105: # only the proof body back into the original, as the model campaign did, drops\\n106: # every lemma a real proof needs and turns a correct proof into \\\"unresolved\\\".\\n107: #\\n108: # Compiling the returned file is only sound if nothing in it can change what is\\n109: # being proved. The checks below close the routes that exist in Lean for that:\\n110: # editing `Candidate` or anything it elaborates against; extending its term past\\n111: # where it ended (a term does not stop at a newline); re-declaring `Candidate` or\\n112: # the target inside a namespace; adding `variable` hypotheses the target silently\\n113: # absorbs; redefining the tokens `Candidate` parses as; and bypassing the kernel.\\n114: # The target's type is then re-checked by Lean itself, and the axiom audit rejects\\n115: # `sorryAx` and anything non-standard.\\n116: \\n117: _CMD_START = (\\\"theorem\\\", \\\"lemma\\\", \\\"def\\\", \\\"abbrev\\\", \\\"example\\\", \\\"instance\\\", \\\"open\\\",\\n118:               \\\"set_option\\\", \\\"attribute\\\", \\\"noncomputable\\\", \\\"private\\\", \\\"protected\\\",\\n119:               \\\"@[\\\", \\\"#check\\\", \\\"#print\\\", \\\"structure\\\", \\\"inductive\\\", \\\"class\\\")\\n120: \\n121: _FORBIDDEN_CMD = re.compile(\\n122:     r\\\"(?m)^\\\\s*(?:(?:private|protected|noncomputable|local|scoped)\\\\s+)*\\\"\\n123:     r\\\"(namespace|section|variable|universe|notation\\\\w*|\\n<truncated 4260 bytes>\\ndate` or target in some\\n212:     other namespace cannot stand in for it.\\n213:     \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n214:     ret = _norm(returned)\\n215:     other = \\\"refute\\\" if direction == \\\"prove\\\" else \\\"prove\\\"\\n216:     header = {\\\"prove\\\": rf\\\"(?m)^theorem\\\\s+prove_{ident}\\\\s*:\\\\s*Candidate\\\\s*:=\\\",\\n217:               \\\"refute\\\": rf\\\"(?m)^theorem\\\\s+refute_{ident}\\\\s*:\\\\s*¬\\\\s*Candidate\\\\s*:=\\\"}\\n218:     pristine = {\\\"prove\\\": f\\\"theorem prove_{ident} : Candidate := by\\\\n  sorry\\\",\\n219:                 \\\"refute\\\": f\\\"theorem refute_{ident} : ¬ Candidate := by\\\\n  sorry\\\"}\\n220:     if not re.search(header[direction], _strip_comments(ret)):\\n221:         return None\\n222:     if pristine[direction] in ret:\\n223:         return None\\n224:     body = ret.replace(pristine[other], \\\"\\\")\\n225:     ty = \\\"_root_.Candidate\\\" if direction == \\\"prove\\\" else \\\"¬ _root_.Candidate\\\"\\n226:     return (body.rstrip() + \\\"\\\\n\\\\n-- verifier: the target must have exactly this type\\\\n\\\"\\n227:             f\\\"theorem agf_verify_{direction} : {ty} := _root_.{direction}_{ident}\\\\n\\\")\\n228: \\n229: \\n230: def do_verify(args) -> int:\\n231:     from pipeline.theorem_prover import LeanSubprocessProver\\n232:     from tools.prover_eval.w16 import is_timeout\\n233: \\n234:     index, src = _index(args.benchmark, args.partition)\\n235:     truth = {}\\n236:     gt = os.path.join(os.path.dirname(args.dir.rstrip(\\\"/\\\")), \\\".ground_truth.json\\\")\\n237:     if os.path.exists(gt):\\n238:         truth = json.load(open(gt))\\n239: \\n240:     CONFIG.lean_timeout_s = args.timeout\\n241:     lean = LeanSubprocessProver(CONFIG)\\n242:     if not lean._available:\\n243:         print(\\\"ERROR: no Lean available — cannot verify.\\\")\\n244:         return 2\\n245: \\n246:     results, counts = [], collections.Counter()\\n247:     for item in index:\\n248:         ident, fname = item[\\\"id\\\"], item[\\\"file\\\"]\\n249:         path = os.path.join(args.dir, fname)\\n250:         if not os.path.exists(path):\\nThe above content does NOT show the entire file contents. If you need to view any lines of the file which were not shown to complete your task, call this tool again to view those lines.\\n\",\"truncated_fields\":[\"content\"]}\n149: {\"step_index\":150,\"source\":\"MODEL\",\"type\":\"PLANNER_RESPONSE\",\"status\":\"DONE\",\"created_at\":\"2026-09-11T18:34:22Z\",\"thinking\":\"**Constructing Theorem Headers & Templates**\\n\\nThe primary task involves generating theorem headers and corresponding templates based on a direction (\\\"prove\\\" or \\\"refute\\\") and an identifier. The code creates regular expressions for both \\\"prove\\\" and \\\"refute\\\" headers, as well as their pristine, unproven template counterparts. \\\"Refute\\\" cases require the addition of the negation symbol.\\n\\n\\n**Analyzing Candidate Goal's Structure**\\n\\nThe goal is a universally quantified statement about graphs. Need to prove the inequality `GraphCalc.radius G + 1 ≤ GraphCalc.romanDominationNumber G` under the given hypotheses. This inequality is likely the core of the proof, so the focus should be on establishing it.\\n\\n\\n**Decomposing Roman Domination Proof**\\n\\nThe plan involves using `le_romanDominationNumber`, which requires showing `radius G + 1 ≤ romanWeight f` for any roman dominating function *f*. Need to explore the meaning of `romanWeight f` and relate it to `radius G`. The key is expressing the relationship between the graph's radius and the sum of function values, W, in the Roman domination context.\\n\\nThe definition of the graph's radius uses eccentricity and `ballWithin`. The `ballWithin` function definition will be critical to relate it to the `romanWeight`.\\n\\n\\n**Relating Radius and Roman Domination**\\n\\nConsidered if there is a center vertex *c* where `ballWithin G (W - 1) c = univ`. Observed that in the example $P_6$, the center was adjacent to a vertex in $S_2$.  Reasoned that every vertex with a zero function value has a neighbor in either $S_2$ or $S_1$, so every vertex is at most distance 1 from $S_2 \\\\cup S_1$. The eccentricity of a center vertex is shown to be $\\\\le W - 1$. The general relationship between *W* and the diameter or radius requires further investigation, so looking at known relations. Analyzed existing inequalities related to domination number and roman domination number from a relevant file.\\n\\n\\n**Exploring Radius and Domination**\\n\\nConsidered the relationship between radius and domination number. Initially tested if radius could be bounded by $2\\\\gamma - 1$. For $\\\\gamma = 1$, the radius is at most 1, derived from a dominating vertex having a closed neighborhood equal to $V$.\\n\\nGeneralized the approach for $\\\\gamma = 2$ and $\\\\gamma = 3$, concluding that $\\\\gamma = 2$ yields a maximum radius of 3, and the Roman domination number is at most 4. For $\\\\gamma=3$ the radius should still be at most 3 with Roman domination $\\\\le 6$.\\n\\n**Re-evaluating Proof Direction**\\n\\nThe initial assumption of a proof strategy may be incorrect. The previous attempts did not definitively rule out a counterexample. Reconsidered if \\\"refute\\\" is the correct direction, given that the random graph testing was not exhaustive.\\n\\n**Testing Counterexample Hypothesis**\\n\\nFocus shifted to determining if a graph exists where the radius plus one is greater than the Roman domination number. Re-evaluated the condition for a counterexample: `radius + 1 > romanDominationNumber`, which simplifies to `radius >= romanDominationNumber`.\\n\\n**Exploring the Case of Equality**\\n\\nInvestigated whether a graph could have `radius = romanDominationNumber`. Observed that if this were true, then `radius + 1 > romanDominationNumber`, confirming the counterexample possibility. The central question is whether a graph can be constructed where this equality holds.\\n\\n\\n\",\"tool_calls\":[{\"name\":\"run_command\",\"args\":{\"CommandLine\":\"\\\"grep -rn \\\\\\\"worker_\\\\\\\" agy.log\\\"\",\"Cwd\":\"\\\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_e20263a9\\\"\",\"WaitMsBeforeAsync\":\"5000\",\"toolAction\":\"\\\"Running command\\\"\",\"toolSummary\":\"\\\"Search worker_ in agy.log\\\"\"}}]}\n150: {\"step_index\":151,\"source\":\"MODEL\",\"type\":\"GENERIC\",\"status\":\"DONE\",\"created_at\":\"2026-09-11T18:34:34Z\",\"content\":\"Created At: 2026-09-11T20:34:34+02:00\\nCompleted At: 2026-09-11T20:34:34+02:00\\n\\nThe command exited with code 1.\\nStdout:\\n\\nStderr:\\n\\n\"}\n151: {\"step_index\":152,\"source\":\"MODEL\",\"type\":\"PLANNER_RESPONSE\",\"status\":\"DONE\",\"created_at\":\"2026-09-11T18:34:34Z\",\"thinking\":\"**Assessing worker failure status**\\n\\nThe swarm has only spawned 4 out of 16 expected subagents. The trajectory shows all launched workers crashed or failed immediately. Worker `0d77a839` is still pending launch. The crash pattern suggests a critical issue preventing worker initialization.\\n\\n\\n**Analyzing proof completion strategy**\\n\\nLemmas `minUpTo_le`, `radius_le_eccentricity`, and `le_romanDominationNumber` are available in `scratch.lean`. The goal is to combine these to prove `prove_e20263a9`. Evaluating if lemmas from `test_ball.lean` are also relevant. The proof structure of `prove_e20263a9` needs to be investigated in relation to the available lemmas.\\n\\n\\n\",\"tool_calls\":[{\"name\":\"run_command\",\"args\":{\"CommandLine\":\"\\\"grep -rn \\\\\\\"def radiu